<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 428–348 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plato was an Athenian philosopher of the fifth and fourth centuries BCE, author of dialogues such as the Republic. His works were preserved in Byzantine libraries. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, émigré scholars from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> helped bring Platonic texts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, fuelling a revival of his thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Socrates, tried and executed in 399 BCE, left his mark through his pupils; <b>Plato</b> founded the Academy around 387 BCE, Aristotle the Lyceum in 335 BCE, and Zeno and Epicurus opened their Stoic and Epicurean schools around 300 BCE, making Athens the philosophical capital of the Mediterranean into…</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…scientific heritage preserved in Byzantine libraries, transmitted to Italy by scholars such as Manuel Chrysoloras from 1397 and by émigrés after the empire's fall, helped shape the Italian Renaissance: the recovery of <b>Plato</b> and the Greek historians in Florence, Venice and Rome was substantially mediated by Byzantine scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Plato</b>'s Apology Socrates traces his philosophical mission to the Pythia's answer that no one was wiser than he.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Heraclitus, with his teaching on the unity of opposites, constant change and the logos, became one of the most influential of the early Greek philosophers, read by <b>Plato</b>, the Stoics and modern thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ephorus and Aristotle compared them with those of Sparta, and <b>Plato</b> set his last dialogue, the Laws, among three old men walking from Knossos to the cave of Zeus on Mount Ida, one of them a Knossian named Kleinias who is charged with founding a new Cretan city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The chancellors Coluccio Salutati and Leonardo Bruni shaped a civic humanism that praised republican liberty, and Marsilio Ficino translated <b>Plato</b> for Cosimo de’ Medici.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-376" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Plato"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-376">What was here in 376 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the truth would be literally nothing but the shadows of the images.&#8221;</p><footer>Republic, book 7 &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; the allegory of the cave; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1497/pg1497-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now the worst part of the punishment is that he who refuses to rule is liable to be ruled by one who is worse than himself.&#8221;</p><footer>Republic, book 1 &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1497/pg1497-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the true creator is necessity, who is the mother of our invention.&#8221;</p><footer>Republic, book 2 &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1497/pg1497-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Until philosophers are kings, or the kings and princes of this world have the spirit and power of philosophy, and political greatness and wisdom meet in one…&#8221;</p><footer>Republic, book 5 &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1497/pg1497-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You know also that the beginning is the most important part of any work, especially in the case of a young and tender thing; for that is the time at which the character is being formed and the desired impression is more readily taken.&#8221;</p><footer>Republic, book 2 &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue, on the education of children &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1497/pg1497-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for wonder is the feeling of a philosopher, and philosophy begins in wonder.&#8221;</p><footer>Theaetetus &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Socrates is the speaker in the dialogue &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1726/pg1726-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
